          "source_text_sha256": "4ac074b18f86d9b47c7557b126165d34fd9670c83af51934e5cdb3fca1a38ccb",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "needs-review",
          "text": "Description: On November 11, 2014, around 6 a.m., a witness, from their caravan by the sea, observed a bright white round object moving slowly at first, then accelerating and making zig-zags along the coast. The observation lasted 20 minutes. The phenomenon disappeared over the horizon. Only one testimony was collected. After analyzing various hypotheses (see the investigation report), including: - a ship moving in the Mont-Saint-Michel bay. - a helicopter flying low along the Granville coast. - an airplane approaching the Granville airport with landing lights on. - an airplane flying over the same area with lights also on. the preferred hypothesis is that of the helicopter (civil security or coast guard) equipped with a spotlight. Therefore, the GEIPAN classifies this case as 'B': probable observation of a helicopter (civil security or coast guard) equipped with a spotlight flying along the Granville coast.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "3d15e2cf5a45f91a6668083e84cc544484de92226044ec25e034674227e5c4c7"
        }
